\version "2.24.0"
% c322 (A&S Unit 25, PDF 435 sec. 17, Ex. 25-32b-c: an ascending 5-6 sequence intensified by a chromatic passing tone that makes the 6 an applied 6/5, the chromatic line strongest in the bass; PDF 419/439: do not double the temporary leading tone or the 7th): 36.4 V6/5 of B flat minor (A natural-C-E flat-F) between A flat (36.3) and B flat minor (37); bass A flat-A-B flat (Cb, Cbsn, Bn 2, B.tbn); Ob 2 holds E flat5 as the prepared 7th and falls to D flat5; Cl 1 and Hn 1 A flat4-F4; countersubject (Vla, Vc, Bn 1) C-F-E flat-C (no octaves with Ob 1/Tbn 1's E flat-F, c212). c212's 5-6 line in Ob 1 and Tbn 1 kept.
% COUNTERSTATEMENT, bars 27-42, ff. Theme F1 in the bass (cellos, violas, bassoons, horns 3-4; basses an octave lower);
% the violins play a running countersubject in eighths (Adler part 2 PDF 172-186: polyphonic texture, foreground and counterline).
% 35-38: the head in the violins in A flat, then B flat minor; 39-42: V of E flat, diminuendo into the transition.
% Woodwinds and horns 1-2 hold the harmony; trombones join at 35. No repeated-eighth hammering in the basses: that layout is
% movement I's first-theme tutti and is kept for the coda (cyclic-notes 'Owed' item 7).

% c032 (harmony; A&S Unit 17, PDF 267: voices above a sequential bass follow the sequence, or 5ths appear): 32 Fl 1, Ob 2, Cl 1 G-F-A flat (were G-A flat, parallel 5ths with the bass statement's C-D flat); 35 Fl 2 and Ob 1 C5 (were E flat5: D-E flat over G-A flat, parallel 5ths in the V-VI move into the A flat statement); 38 Fl 2 and Ob 1 C5-D5, Cl 1 E flat4-D4 takes the seventh (were D flat-E flat, 5ths with Vln II's G flat-A flat).
% c132 (harmony; A&S Unit 22, PDF 377 and 383 point 4: a seventh enters as passing tone, common tone or neighbour): 37 Cl 1 B flat4 dotted half-F4 on beat 4 (after the violins' B flat-F, so no octaves with them), so the 7th E flat4 of ii7 at 38 comes by step from F (was a leap of a 5th from B flat4).
% c208 (expression; Rimsky-Korsakov ch. IV, PDF 129-130 and Ex. 254, PDF 418: a note is stressed by a chain of three or four small unstressed notes in scale form, an upward glide slurred to it, in strings or wood-wind, not into string chords of three or four notes): 35 Vln I-II (unison, single notes) glide E flat-F-G into A flat 5, the head of F1 on VI, the counterstatement's first new key, so the violins' change from countersubject (27-34) to theme is heard; F1's notes and rhythm unchanged, Fl 1 keeps the plain note. Not at 37: a glide F-G-A flat into B flat 5 makes outer-voice octaves A flat-B flat with the bass (36.3-37).
% c208 (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 136: after a long rest the trombones should re-enter pp or ff, 'piano and forte re-entries are less successful'; PDF 129: sf-dim): 35 Tbn 1-2, B.tbn (silent since bar 6) and the Cbsn they double attack ff and fall to f within bar 35 (the f on beat 4, so bar 36 carries no new mark); the timpani stroke stays f.
% c212 (harmony; A&S Unit 17, PDF 265-266 Ex. 17-2c, PDF 273-275 secs. 11 and 17-10/17-11: two root-position triads a step apart need a voice-leading chord, and the ascending 5-6
% syncope supplies it; the 6 falls on the weak beat and is tied over, PDF 273; PDF 267: repeated patterns otherwise give 5ths and octaves): 36.4 Fl 2, Ob 1 and Tbn 1 rise E flat -> F (5-6 over A flat,
% ii6 of E flat) and tie the F into 37 as the fifth of B flat minor, then fall to D flat on 37.3. Ob 2, Cl 1 and Hn 4 no longer move A flat -> B flat in octaves with the bass A flat -> B flat
% (36.3 -> 37.1): Ob 2 and Hn 4 follow Bn 1 to D flat (Ob 2 then D flat 5 - C5 - B flat 4 at 38), Cl 1 holds F4 and still steps F -> E flat into 38 (c132); Hn 2 takes C4 at 38.1 (was A flat 3: its B flat 3 -> A flat 3 made fifths with Cl 1's F4 -> E flat 4 across 37-38).
% c237 (texture; Adler ch. 16, adler2 PDF 201 consideration 3: a diminuendo is made clearer when written out in the score; PDF 213: Dvorak, Slavonic Dance 8 m. 33, 'a model of exactly how one accomplishes a diminuendo from fortissimo to piano', and Bizet, La Toupie mm. 1-5 (Ex. XVI-6, PDF 202): the diminuendo 'clearly orchestrated, instead of being left to the players', by drop-outs; PDF 229: two colours at the unison swallow each other):
% 39-42 the held V7 of E flat thins out by stages instead of 17 wind and string parts all holding ff > p: Hn 3-4, Ob 1 and the bass trombone stop after 40 with the trombones and Cbsn (Hn 3-4 doubled Hn 1-2 at the unison, Ob 1 doubled Fl 2's D5); Ob 2, Cl 1, Cl 2 and Hn 1 stop after 41 (Cl 1 and Hn 1 doubled the violins' falling F4 at the unison, Cl 2 doubled Hn 2's B flat3). At 42 Fl 1 A flat5 (unchanged), Fl 2 D5, Bn 1 D4, Hn 2 B flat3, Bn 2, strings: the transition's pp group (Bn 1, Vln II, Vla, Vc, Cb) is what is left. Pitches unchanged.
% c431 (melody; Adler part 2 ch. 11, PDF 28-29, Ex. XI-12 Schubert Rosamunde: two trombones drop out and the third stays with the lower strings' line, scoring the diminuendo; PDF 30-32 Berlioz, soft trombones as melodic colour): 41-42 Tbn 1 keeps its F3 from 40 and plays F1's last fragment F3-D3-E flat3-F3 with the cellos, falling to p; Tbn 2 and the bass trombone still stop after 40.
% c391 (melody; Adler ch. 3, adler1 PDF 73-74: open strings stand out from stopped notes and are avoided in soft expressive phrases; PDF 74, Ex. III-5: the G string is the thickest, most sonorous string;
%   PDF 76: a phrase meant for one string is marked sul G; PDF 99: the cello's G string is the least strong): 41-42 F1's last fragment F4-D4-E flat4-F4 (violins) and F3-D3-E flat3-F3 (cellos), ff > p,
%   is marked sul G, so the dotted D is not the open D string in all violins and cellos while the line fades into the transition; violins in 3rd position, cellos in 4th. Notes, slurs, dynamics unchanged.
% c492 (harmony; A&S Unit 23, PDF 387-388 sec. 2, Ex. 23-2d (II with flat 6), PDF 395 points 2a and 3 (flat 6 colours II7 and goes to 5); PDF 387: the 6-5 of minor): 38.1-2 the ii7 before the cadential 6/4 of E flat minor is now ii-half-dim7 F-A flat-C flat-E flat (Vln I-II C flat 6, Vla and Bn 1 C flat 4, Ob 1-2 C flat 5, Cl 2 C flat 4, Tbn 1-2 C flat 3), so the bar is in one mode with the 6/4's G flat; every C flat falls to B flat (Ob 1-2 and Cl 2 on 38.4, Tbn on 38.3; Cl 2 B flat 3-D4 in eighths, so its D4 at 39 does not double Ob 1 and Tbn 2's B flat-D in octaves). F1's head, bass and dynamics unchanged.
pTwoVlnOne = { c'''8(\ff bes'' aes'' g'') f''( d'' b' d'') | bes'8( d'' f'' aes'') c'''( aes'' f'' c'') | b'8( d'' f'' d'') g''( f'' es'' d'') | b'8( c'' d'' f'') es''( g'' c''' es''') | d'''8( c''' bes'' g'') es''( g'' bes'' es''') | des'''8( c''' bes'' aes'') f''( aes'' c''' f''') | aes''8( g'' f'' es'') d''( f'' aes'' c''') | b''8( c''' d''' b'' g''4) r4 | \grace { es''16( f'' g'' } aes''2-> es''4. f''8 | g''4-.) f''8( es''8 des''4-.) c''4-. | f'''8(\f es''' des''' c''') bes''( des''' f''' bes'') | ces'''8( aes'' f'' aes'') ges''( es''' f''' d''') | f''2->(\ff\> bes'4. c''8 | d''4-.) c''8( bes'8 aes'4-.) g'4-. | \once \override TextSpanner.bound-details.left.text = \markup \italic "sul G" f'2(\startTextSpan d'4. es'8 | f'2.)\p\stopTextSpan r4 | }
% c097 (texture; Adler ch. 11, PDF 42-44, Hindemith Symphonic Metamorphoses ii 158-196: every instrument in its best register 'so that the subject comes through clearly every time'; the voices around the subject drop to p): 27-38 Vln II plays the countersubject at Vln I's pitch. Its lower octave (B flat3-E flat5) ran through the bass statement's register (C4-A flat4 in Hn 3-4, bassoons, violas, cellos), crossing under its F4 peak at 30 and meeting it at the unison at 28, 29, 30 and 33; at 35-38 it lay under the violas' and cellos' countersubject. The lower octave returns at 39. 27 the woodwind and Hn 1-2 pads mf (were f) under the ff subject and countersubject.
pTwoVlnTwo = { c'''8(\ff -\tag #'part ^\markup \italic "unis. with Vln I" bes'' aes'' g'') f''( d'' b' d'') | bes'8( d'' f'' aes'') c'''( aes'' f'' c'') | b'8( d'' f'' d'') g''( f'' es'' d'') | b'8( c'' d'' f'') es''( g'' c''' es''') | d'''8( c''' bes'' g'') es''( g'' bes'' es''') | des'''8( c''' bes'' aes'') f''( aes'' c''' f''') | aes''8( g'' f'' es'') d''( f'' aes'' c''') | b''8( c''' d''' b'' g''4) r4 | \grace { es''16( f'' g'' } aes''2-> es''4. f''8 | g''4-.) f''8( es''8 des''4-.) c''4-. | f'''8(\f es''' des''' c''') bes''( des''' f''' bes'') | ces'''8( aes'' f'' aes'') ges''( es''' f''' d''') | f'2->(\ff\> bes4. c'8 | d'4-.) c'8( bes8 aes4-.) g4-. | \once \override TextSpanner.bound-details.left.text = \markup \italic "sul G" f'2(\startTextSpan d'4. es'8 | f'2.)\p\stopTextSpan r4 | }
% c314 (orchestration; Adler ch. 15, adler2 PDF 176: in the Jupiter coda each rendition of the cantus firmus is supported by one member of the string choir; PDF 177-178: in the Meistersinger combination each element has at least one string, one woodwind and one brass colour, the second theme with octave-lower cellos; PDF 178-179, Ex. XV-27: Scheherazade's theme in cellos and horns; PDF 165: octave doubling for power is clearer than unison):
%   37-38 the cellos leave the countersubject (which keeps Vln I-II, violas and Bn 1) and double Hn 3-4's F1 head an octave below, ff, with the horns' articulation, so the head is no longer a brass-only line inside the strings' eighths; 39 cellos f > with the violas and basses. Notes of the head unchanged.
%   38.3 Cl 2 holds C4 to beat 4 (9-8 over B flat, as Fl 2 and the oboes since c297), so its C4-B flat 3 makes no fifths with the cellos' F3-E flat 3.
% c344 (Adler ch. 5, PDF 141, Ex. V-19, Shostakovich 6/iii: when the violins come down into their low register the cellos double them an octave
%   below for "greater resolution", just before a woodwind scale interlude): at 41-42 the violins' F4-D4-Eb4-F4 (unison, > p) is doubled by the
%   cellos at F3-D3-Eb3-F3. The B flat pedal stays in Cb (B flat1, held into 43 since c329), Bn 2 (B flat2) and Hn 2 (B flat3); c237's drop-outs unchanged.
pTwoVc = { \clef tenor c'2->(\ff g4. aes8 | bes4-.) aes8( g8 f4-.) es4-. | d4( g8 aes8 b4 d'4) | f'2-> es'4( d'4) | es'2->( bes4. c'8 | des'4-.) c'8( bes8 aes4-.) g4-. | f4( aes8 c'8 f'4 aes'4) | g'2.-> r4 | es'8( des'8 c'8 bes8) aes8( c'8 es'8 aes'8) | bes'8( g'8 es'8 des'8) c'8( f'8 es'8 c'8) | bes2->(\ff f4. ges8 | aes4-.) ges8( f8 es4-.) d4-. | \clef bass bes,1\f\> | bes,1 | \once \override TextSpanner.bound-details.left.text = \markup \italic "sul G" f2(\startTextSpan d4. es8 | f2.)\p\stopTextSpan r4 | }
pTwoVla = { c'2->(\ff g4. aes8 | bes4-.) aes8( g8 f4-.) es4-. | d4( g8 aes8 b4 d'4) | f'2-> es'4( d'4) | es'2->( bes4. c'8 | des'4-.) c'8( bes8 aes4-.) g4-. | f4( aes8 c'8 f'4 aes'4) | g'2.-> r4 | es'8( des'8 c'8 bes8) aes8( c'8 es'8 aes'8) | bes'8( g'8 es'8 des'8) c'8( f'8 es'8 c'8) | f'8(\f es'8 des'8 c'8) bes8( des'8 f'8 bes8) | \stemDown ces'8( aes8 f8 aes8) \stemNeutral ges8( es'8 f'8 d'8) | d'1\> | d'1 | aes1 | f2.\p r4 | }
% c277 (texture; Adler ch. 8, adler1 PDF 267-268 Ex. VIII-39 (Dvorak, Carnival: the bassoons double the cellos at pitch in a tutti), PDF 255 (the melody must come through the harmony), PDF 257 (a unison doubling of a held note only strengthens the pitch), PDF 271 (rhythmic wind counterpoint)):
%   35-38 Bn 1 follows the violas from F1 (27-34) into the countersubject (35-38) instead of holding A flat3-G3-D flat4-C4, which Hn 4 and Hn 3 already hold at the unison; the eighth-note line, alone in the strings under ten held wind and brass parts in its register, gets a reed colour as the violins' countersubject had Cl 1-2 at 27-34.
%   35 Hn 3-4 f (were ff from F1's bass statement) on their held chord tones C4/A flat3, the countersubject's register; the trombones already fall to f in 35 (c208), Hn 1-2 are mf (Adler PDF 268, Ex. VIII-40: keep the brass down so the strings and winds come through).
% c306 (Adler adler1 PDF 225: bassoon in tenor clef 'from about the G above middle C upward' when ledger lines accumulate): Bn 1 part only, tenor clef 33-38 (eighths up to B flat 4, four ledger lines in bass clef); bass clef again at 39. Score unchanged (shared staff with Bn 2's A flat 2).
% c365 (Gould, Behind Bars ch. 1, PDF 21: bassoon, trombone and cello use the tenor clef; stay in one clef only while at most three ledger lines are needed; PDF 22: change clef between phrases):
% 33-38 the shared bassoon staff takes the tenor clef in the score too (part-only since c306). In bass clef Bn 1's eighths at 33-36 reached A flat4-B flat4 on 3-4 ledger lines while the cellos playing the
% same notes were in tenor clef; in tenor Bn 1 needs at most two ledger lines (B flat4) and Bn 2's A flat2/B flat2 at 35-38 one or two. Clefs at the phrase start 33 and at 39, where the cellos also return to bass. No sound change.
% c545 (Gould, Behind Bars PDF 21: for performance material stay in one clef using up to at least three ledger lines, which shows the contour;
%   PDF 22: in continuous playing put the new clef between phrases, and between beats after rests): the bassoons' tenor clef moves from 33 to 35.
%   33-34 are bars 7-8 of the F1 statement 27-34 that both bassoons play a2 with the cellos, not a phrase start; in bass clef they need at most three
%   ledger lines (A flat4 at 33.4, G4 at 34). The clef now follows the rest at 34.4, before the sequence of eighths at 35-38 (B flat4) that c365 gave it for.
%   Gould PDF 22: 'the clef always goes before the barline'. At 35 Fl 1 and Vln I-II start with three grace sixteenths, so a clef at the main beat of 35
%   printed after the barline and the grace column (the basses' bass clef at 35 had done so since c016). Both clefs at 35 (Bn, Cb) now sit at the grace
%   moment, \grace { \clef ... s16 s16 s16 }, and print before the barline. Spacer graces only; MIDI unchanged.
pTwoBsnOne = { c'2->(\ff g4. aes8 | bes4-.) aes8( g8 f4-.) es4-. | d4( g8 aes8 b4 d'4) | f'2-> es'4( d'4) | es'2->( bes4. c'8 | des'4-.) c'8( bes8 aes4-.) g4-. | f4( aes8 c'8 f'4 aes'4) | g'2.-> r4 | \grace { \clef tenor s16 s16 s16 } es'8( des'8 c'8 bes8) aes8( c'8 es'8 aes'8) | bes'8( g'8 es'8 des'8) c'8( f'8 es'8 c'8) | f'8(\f es'8 des'8 c'8) bes8( des'8 f'8 bes8) | ces'8( aes8 f8 aes8) ges8( es'8 f'8 d'8) | \clef bass d'1~\> | d'1~ | d'1~ | d'2.\p r4 | }
% c387 (Rimsky-Korsakov ch. II, PDF 76-78: a wind in unison with a string group increases its resonance, 'Cellos + Fag.' is a best pair, and in a melody in octaves each octave takes its own doubling; PDF 79: horns and cellos blend): 37-38 Bn 2 doubles the cellos' B flat-minor F1 head (B flat3) at the unison, f like Bn 1 on the shared staff, so the lower octave under Hn 3-4's ff B flat4 is no longer the cellos alone (as 99-100 and 103-104, where Bn 2 doubles the cellos' head). 36 beat 4 rests (the A2 stays in Cb, Cbsn, B. tbn) to breathe before the entry; 39-42 unchanged.
pTwoBsnTwo = { c'2->(\ff g4. aes8 | bes4-.) aes8( g8 f4-.) es4-. | d4( g8 aes8 b4 d'4) | f'2-> es'4( d'4) | es'2->( bes4. c'8 | des'4-.) c'8( bes8 aes4-.) g4-. | f4( aes8 c'8 f'4 aes'4) | g'2.-> r4 | aes,1 | es2 aes,4 r4 | bes2->(\f f4. ges8 | aes4-.) ges8( f8 es4-.) d4-. | bes,1~\> | bes,1~ | bes,1~ | bes,2.\p r4 | }
% c297 (texture; Adler ch. 11, adler2 PDF 41: contrapuntal brass writing means imitation beyond the antiphonal exchange of blocks; PDF 44 and Ex. XI-24, PDF 45-47 (Bartok, Concerto for Orchestra i 313-386): the subject passes from one group to the brass, which enter 'f, ben marc.', while the group that had it goes on in free counterpoint; PDF 44: two horns in unison weigh as one trumpet; PDF 42, Ex. XI-23: each brass entry in the instrument's best register):
%   37-38 F1's head in B flat minor moves from the violins and Fl 1 to Hn 3-4 a2 at B flat 4, ff ben marc. (the register and players of the development's answer at 103), and Vln I-II go on with the countersubject two octaves above the violas, cellos and Bn 1, f, so 35-40 is a dialogue strings - horns - strings instead of three violin statements over held brass. Hn 3-4 rest on 36.3-4 (breath; Hn 4's A flat 3 -> B flat 4 made octaves with the bass A flat -> B flat); Fl 1 holds B flat 5 - A flat 5 - B flat 5 into 39; 38.3 Fl 2, Ob 1, Ob 2 hold C5 to beat 4 (9-8 over B flat, with the head's 4-3 E flat-D), so the head's F4-E flat 4 makes no fifths with their C5-B flat 4. F1's notes unchanged; only its instrument and octave at 37-38.
% c298 (expression; A&S Unit 21, PDF 348 sec. 4: the voice that carries a suspension comes forward, and performers accent the suspension and play its resolution softer; PDF 350 sec. 8: the cadential 4-3 over V
%   stresses the leading tone; PDF 367-368 sec. 28: a pedal is the static substructure under the moving voices; adler2 PDF 44: two horns in unison weigh as one trumpet):
%   c297 gave 37-38 to Hn 3-4 a2 ff and set the violins' countersubject f, but left the same countersubject ff in the violas, cellos and Bn 1 in the horns' register (E flat 4-A flat 4),
%   and it left the violins at f and Hn 3-4 at ff for 39, where the violins take the head back over the held B flat pedal chord. 37 Vla, Vc, Bn 1 (and Bn 2, shared staff) f with the violins, so the
%   horns' head and its cadential 4-3 (E flat 4-D4 over B flat, 38.3-4) lead; 39 Vln I-II ff > (their level until v297), Hn 3-4 f > (c277's level), so the head is the loudest line over the held chord. Notes unchanged.
pTwoHnThree = { c'2->(\ff g4. aes8 | bes4-.) aes8( g8 f4-.) es4-. | d4( g8 aes8 b4 d'4) | f'2-> es'4( d'4) | es'2->( bes4. c'8 | des'4-.) c'8( bes8 aes4-.) g4-. | f4( aes8 c'8 f'4 aes'4) | g'2.-> r4 | c'1\f | bes2 r2 | bes'2->(\ff^\markup \italic "ben marc." f'4. ges'8 | aes'4-.) ges'8( f'8 es'4-.) d'4-. | f'1~\f\> | f'1 | R1*2\! | }
pTwoHnFour = { c'2->(\ff g4. aes8 | bes4-.) aes8( g8 f4-.) es4-. | d4( g8 aes8 b4 d'4) | f'2-> es'4( d'4) | es'2->( bes4. c'8 | des'4-.) c'8( bes8 aes4-.) g4-. | f4( aes8 c'8 f'4 aes'4) | g'2.-> r4 | aes1\f | g2 r2 | bes'2->(\ff^\markup \italic "ben marc." f'4. ges'8 | aes'4-.) ges'8( f'8 es'4-.) d'4-. | bes1~\f\> | bes1 | R1*2\! | }
% c166 (Rimsky-Korsakov ch. I, PDF 26: the double bass's practical top is G (written); higher notes with caution and never by skip): 33 beat 4 basses repeat F3 (written F4) instead of leaping to A flat 3 (written A flat 4); the A flat stays in the cellos, violas, bassoons and Hn 3-4.
pTwoCb = { \clef tenor c2->(\ff g,4. aes,8 | bes,4-.) aes,8( g,8 f,4-.) es,4-. | d,4( g,8 aes,8 b,4 d4) | f2-> es4( d4) | es2->( bes,4. c8 | des4-.) c8( bes,8 aes,4-.) g,4-. | f,4( aes,8 c8 f4 f4) | g2.-> r4 | \grace { \clef bass s16 s16 s16 } aes,,1 | es,2 aes,,4 a,,4 | bes,,1 | f,2 bes,,2 | bes,,1\> | bes,,1 | bes,,1 | bes,,1\p\>~ | }
% c075 (MOLA Example 3, PDF 10): the contrabassoon re-enters at 35 after two bars' rest at f, with the bass trombone it doubles
% c187 (Rimsky-Korsakov ch. II, PDF 77: D. basses + Bass cl. among the best unison pairs; PDF 80, Ex. 96 and Servilia 168: the bass octave of a low melody
% doubled by a reed): 27-34 the bass clarinet doubles the basses' lower octave of the bass statement (C3), which was the basses alone under six players at C4.
% 33 it takes the cellos' A flat (the basses repeat F, c166). The bass clarinet is otherwise silent from 7 to 143.
pTwoBassCl = { c2->(\ff g,4. aes,8 | bes,4-.) aes,8( g,8 f,4-.) es,4-. | d,4( g,8 aes,8 b,4 d4) | f2-> es4( d4) | es2->( bes,4. c8 | des4-.) c8( bes,8 aes,4-.) g,4-. | f,4( aes,8 c8 f4 aes4) | g2.-> r4 | R1*8 | }
pTwoCbsn = { c,2->(\ff g,,4. aes,,8 | bes,,4-.) aes,,8( g,,8 f,,4-.) es,,4-. | d,,4( g,,8 aes,,8 b,,4 d,4) | f,2-> es,4( d,4) | es,2->( bes,,4. c,8 | des,4-.) c,8( bes,,8 aes,,4-.) g,,4-. | R1*2 | aes,,2.~\ff\> aes,,4\f | es,2 aes,,4 a,,4 | bes,,1 | f,2 bes,,2 | bes,,1~\> | bes,,1 | R1*2\! | }
% c188 (Adler ch. 10, adler1 PDF 309 and 321: slurred notes are one breath, separate notes are tongued; Ex. X-15, X-16, PDF 306-307: a sustained soft horn/wind choir is held, not re-attacked):
% held pitches in the wind and brass pads are tied over the barline (28-34) and through the staged diminuendo 39-42 (re-attacked once, at 39, where it starts), so the B flat pedal fades without four fresh attacks; Hn 1-2 breathe after 38. Notes and dynamics unchanged.
% c201 (melody; Rimsky-Korsakov ch. II, PDF 76-77: Vln + Fl is a natural unison and the strings predominate; PDF 67: doublings belong to loud passages; PDF 54: a melody stands out as the top part): 35-38 F1's head (Vln I+II unison, ff) lay under Fl 1's held chord tones and was not the top line. Fl 1 now doubles the violins' head at the unison, f, with their articulation; Fl 2 keeps its held notes, marked f with it (shared staff). The octave-above layout of PDF 78 cost a page.
% c209 (Rimsky-Korsakov ch. II, PDF 76-77: a wood-wind in unison with a string line amplifies it only when it plays the same line; Ex. 3, PDF 177, and Ex. 12, PDF 186: the flute doubling a violin melody follows its ornaments): 35 Fl 1 (c201's unison doubling of F1's head with Vln I-II) takes the violins' grace glide E flat-F-G (c208), so the doubled attack on A flat5 is one sound, not a plain flute note against the violins' glide. Fl 2 gets matching grace skips (shared staff). Notes otherwise unchanged.
% c222 (harmony; A&S Unit 22, PDF 373-375, Ex. 22-1 (Beethoven op. 10/1, C minor, bars 10 and 12) and Points for Review 1, PDF 383: VII°4/3, like V4/2, stands on the upper neighbour of I6; PDF 381: the 7th of °7 as upper neighbour to 5, 5-6-5; box PDF 381: dissonances involving the bass resolve, d5 resolves inward; PDF 375, Ex. 22-3c: a d5 that moves to a P5 in similar motion gives obtrusive hidden 5ths, avoid):
%   30 beats 1-2 Fl 1 and Ob 2 A flat 5/A flat 4 (were G held from 29), falling to G on beat 3, so V4/2 over the bass statement's F becomes VII°4/3 (B-D-F-A flat) resolving to i6 (bass E flat): the movement's first °7 with the 4th degree in the bass, the 5-6-5 G-A flat-G that F1's own head carries (G-A flat) now in the winds' top line. 30 Hn 1 F4 resolves to E flat 4 on beat 3 (was G4: Hn 2 B3-Hn 1 F4, a d5, went to the P5 C4-G4 in similar motion), then G4 at 31. Bass statement, countersubject and all other parts unchanged.
% c305 (Gould, Behind Bars ch. 2, PDF 66: keep noteheads separate; a semibreve must not appear joined to a stem; editions reverse the order so the semibreve is never beside a stem): score-only force-hshift at 32 (Fl 1-2) puts the semibreve on the stem-free side of the other part's note, 0.15-0.2 space apart
% c306 (playability, registers; Adler ch. 7, adler1 PDF 184-185 Ex. VII-2: each instrument is given the part that sounds best on it; the flute's middle octave is 'sweet with little carrying power' and cannot be made louder,
%   its upper octave is 'clear and brilliant' and agile, PDF 187 Ex. VII-7; PDF 210 Ex. VII-56: the clarinet's written D6-G6 is 'piercing, shrill'; PDF 174: intonation is hardest at the top; PDF 192: two flutes 'relieve one another' in long fast passages):
%   27-34 Fl 1-2 a2 take the violins' countersubject at pitch (C6-F6 brilliant octave), f; their old mf pad (G5/E flat 5 ..., the same notes as Ob 2 an octave lower and Ob 1 in unison) is gone;
%   Fl 2 breathes on 28.4.5 (the violins keep the eighth); in 30 Fl 1 holds A flat5-G5 above the line (the old pad's notes, no crossing) and breathes on 30.4, so the countersubject's F5-E flat5 is not the top voice in octaves with the bass F-E flat. Cl 1-2 rest 27-34 (c217's a2 doubling lay at written D6-G6 for half its notes, after 16 bars of eighths at 11-26).
% c311 (lens 1; Adler ch. 7, PDF 185: the flute's middle octave is 'sweet and with little carrying power' and a weak register must not be covered;
%   PDF 192: two flutes may relieve one another; PDF 188: the flute needs breath and rest, Brahms 1/iv 38-46 lets Fl 2 hold the long notes): 35-38 Fl 2 held
%   C5-E flat5/F5-D flat5-C5/B flat4 at f, note for note Ob 1's pad (except 36.1-2) under ff strings and the horns' F1 head, inaudible and unbroken since 27.
%   Fl 2 rests 35-38 and re-enters f on D5 at 39, taking the held D5 over from Ob 1 (which stops at 41); Fl 1 restates f at 39 so the shared staff has equal dynamics.
% c394 (Adler part 2 PDF 151-152, Beethoven 9/i Ex. XV-4: the flute superimposes the top octave and needs no doubling there; PDF 151, Mozart Ex. XV-3: an instrument at the unison
%   with heavy strings adds nothing): 35-36 Fl 1 plays F1's A flat-major head and the grace glide an octave above Vln I-II (A flat6-C6), not at their unison under the ff trombone re-entry; 36.4 C6 now steps to the held B flat5 at 37.
% c486 (playability; Adler ch. 7, adler1 PDF 188, Ex. VII-9 Brahms 1/iv 38-46: the second flute holds the long note so the first can breathe, dovetailed; PDF 192: flutes relieve one another
%   in sustained passages): 37-38 Fl 2 (resting since 34) takes the held B flat5-A flat5-B flat5 with Fl 1 at 37.1; Fl 1 stops at 37.3 and re-enters on 38.3's B flat5 under Fl 2's attack,
%   tied into 39 as before, so Fl 1 has a breath in its 35-42 run (11 s at f after 19-34) and the line sounds unbroken. Fl 2 then moves to its D5 at 39 (its f now carried from 37). Pitches heard unchanged.
pTwoFluteOne = { c'''8(\f bes'' aes'' g'') f''( d'' b' d'') | bes'8( d'' f'' aes'') c'''( aes'' f'' c'') | b'8( d'' f'' d'') g''( f'' es'' d'') | aes''2 g''4 r4 | d'''8( c''' bes'' g'') es''( g'' bes'' es''') | des'''8( c''' bes'' aes'') f''( aes'' c''' f''') | aes''8( g'' f'' es'') d''( f'' aes'' c''') | b''8( c''' d''' b'' g''4) r4 | \grace { es'''16( f''' g''' } aes'''2->\f es'''4. f'''8 | g'''4-.) f'''8( es'''8 des'''4-.) c'''4-. | \partCombineApart bes''2\f r2 | r2 bes''2~-\tag #'part \f | \partCombineAutomatic bes''1 | aes''1~\> | aes''1~ | aes''1\p | }
% c454 (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 90 rule 4, PDF 91: concords, not discords, in a pair of one colour): Fl 2 moves D5 -> F5 at 40, a third under Fl 1's held A-flat5 instead of a tritone, while the chord thins out to 42.
pTwoFluteTwo = { c'''8(\f bes'' aes'' g'') f''( d'' b' d'') | bes'8( d'' f'' aes'') c'''( aes'' f'') r8 | b'8( d'' f'' d'') g''( f'' es'' d'') | b'8( c'' d'' f'') es''( g'' c''' es''') | d'''8( c''' bes'' g'') es''( g'' bes'' es''') | des'''8( c''' bes'' aes'') f''( aes'' c''' f''') | aes''8( g'' f'' es'') d''( f'' aes'' c''') | b''8( c''' d''' b'' g''4) r4 | \grace { s16 s16 s16 } R1 | R1 | bes''1\f | aes''2 bes''2 | d''1 | f''1~\> | f''1~ | f''1\p | }
% c309 (lens 9, regression since v299; Rimsky-Korsakov ch. II, PDF 54: a melody stands out by its position at the top; PDF 65 and 67: flutes placed below the oboes give 'a far-fetched, unnatural tone' and confuse the registers):
%   since c306 Fl 1-2 double the violins' countersubject at 27-34, and its low points (B4 at 27.4, B flat4 at 28.1, B4 at 29.1, B4-C5 at 30.1) fell under Ob 1's held mf D5,
%   so the oboe pad sat above the flutes' line four times. Ob 1 rests 27.3-30.2 (Ob 2 and Hn 1-2 keep the pad) and re-enters mf on E flat5 at 30.3, under the line again.
pTwoOboeOne = { es''2\mf r2 | R1*2 | r2 es''2~\mf | es''1 | f''1~ | f''2 d''2~ | d''2. r4 | c''1 | des''2 es''4 f''4~ | f''2 des''2 | ces''2. bes'4 | d''1~ | d''1\> | R1*2\! | }
pTwoOboeTwo = { g'1\mf | bes'2 aes'2 | g'1 | aes'2 g'2~ | g'1 | f'2 aes'2~ | aes'1 | g'2. r4 | aes'1 | g'2 es''2 | des''1 | ces''2. bes'4~ | bes'1 | aes'1~\> | aes'1 | R1\! | }
% c217 (texture; Adler ch. 15, PDF 178: in Wagner's combination each element has at least one string, one woodwind and one brass colour, the violin theme doubled at pitch by Clarinet I; PDF 172: wind doubling gives the violins a sharper edge): 27-34 Cl 1-2 a2 double the violins' countersubject at pitch, f (they held G4-F4-A flat 4 and C4-B3-B flat 3, the same notes as Hn 1 and Hn 2), so the countersubject has a wind colour like the F1 bass under it; a2 keeps one voice on the staff, so page 2 still holds 14-36.
pTwoClOne = { R1*8 | aes'1\mf | g'2 aes'4 f'4~ | f'1 | es'2. f'4~ | f'1~ | f'1~\> | f'1 | R1\! | }
% c529 (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 108: 'Clarinets, on account of their tone quality should rarely be set inside the horns'; PDF 106: horns + clarinets in unison, the wood-wind softens the horn tone; bassoons may sit inside the horns): 38.4-41 Cl 2 holds B flat 3 at the unison with Hn 2 instead of rising to D4 inside the horn pair B flat 3-F4; the third stays in Bn 1 (inside the horns) and the violas. c492's C flat 4 -> B flat 3 on 38.4 kept; a held B flat 3 makes no octaves with Ob 1 and Tbn 2 at 38.4 -> 39.
pTwoClTwo = { R1*8 | c'1\mf | bes2 c'2 | bes1 | ces'2. bes4~ | bes1~ | bes1~\> | bes1 | R1\! | }
% c166 (playability; Rimsky-Korsakov ch. I, PDF 37 and 44: wind and brass players must be given a little rest from time to time): 34 Hn 1-2 rest on beat 4 with every other part (they played 11-42 with only the rests at 18.4 and 26.3-4, 27-42 being 23 s of held notes).
% c299 (Casanelles, Hyperorchestra ch. 6, PDF 271-272 Fig. 6.1: Mahler clears the woodwinds before the soft brass call enters, so it sounds in a free space;
%   PDF 277-278: ensemble timbre depends on 'our psychoacoustic capacity to discern content within a certain frequency area', and a doubling at the unison in that area
%   merges into one sound): since c297 Hn 3-4 carry F1's head at B flat 4 at 37-38, but Hn 1-2 still held F4/B flat 3 (and G flat 4-F4) in the same instrument and octave,
%   so the head's F4 at 37.3 met Hn 1's held F4 and its G flat 4-F4 at 38.2 was answered by the same pitches in Hn 1 at 38.3. Hn 1-2 rest 37-38.2 (the clarinets already hold
%   B flat 3/F4 at 37) and re-enter mf on 38.3 with the 6-5 of the cadential 6/4 (G flat 4-F4) under the head's 4-3; 39 unchanged.
% c299 (Casanelles PDF 274, the harmonic series: octave and fifth at the bottom, the third only higher up; Rimsky PDF 85; c264 at 215-218): 38.1-2 Tbn 2 C3 (was A flat 2,
%   a minor third above the bass trombone's, contrabassoon's and basses' F2 at f), in unison with Tbn 1; A flat stays in the head and the countersubject.
% c542 (A&S Unit 5, PDF 76, Ex. 5-4: the leading tone of V or its inversions is never doubled; inversions of seventh chords are usually complete; Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 83): at 29.3 the bass statement reaches B (V6/5) while Hn 2 held B3 from 29.1, so the chord was B-B-F-G, leading tone doubled and no fifth. Hn 2 now takes D4 on 29.3 only (complete V6/5 B-D-F-G; Hn 1-2 a third F4/D4 for that beat) and returns to B3 on 29.4, a voice exchange with the theme's B3-D4, tied into 30 for c222's VII 4/3 and then C4 as before. D4 on 29.3 to B3 on 30.1 would move in octaves with the countersubject's D5-B4, so the return is on 29.4.
pTwoHnOne = { g'1\mf | f'1~ | f'1~ | f'2 es'2 | g'1 | f'1~ | f'1 | g'2. r4 | aes'1 | g'2 aes'4 f'4 | R1 | r2 ges'4\mf f'4 \breathe | f'1~\> | f'1~ | f'1\p | R1 | }
pTwoHnTwo = { c'2\mf b2 | bes2 c'2 | b2 d'4 b4~ | b2 c'2~ | c'2 bes2 | des'2 c'2~ | c'1 | b2. r4 | c'1 | bes2 c'2 | R1 | r2 bes2\mf \breathe | bes1~\> | bes1~ | bes1~\p | bes1 | }
% c225 (MOLA PDF 5: logical, audible cues during long rests; Example 4, PDF 10): the trombones rest 7-34 (28 bars) and re-enter ff at 35 (c208); part-only cue of the cellos' F1 bass statement 33-34. Score and MIDI unchanged.
pTwoTbnOne = { R1*6 | \tag #'score { R1*2 } \tag #'part { \cueDuring "vc" #UP { R1*2^\markup \tiny "Vc." } } | es2.~\ff\> es4~\f | es2 es4 f4~ | f2 des2 | ces2 bes,4 d4 | f1~\> | f1 | f2( d4. es8 | f2.)\p r4 | }
pTwoTbnTwo = { R1 | R1 | R1 | R1 | R1 | R1 | \tag #'score { R1*2 } \tag #'part { \cueDuring "vc" #UP { R1*2^\markup \tiny "Vc." } } | c2.~\ff\> c4\f | bes,2 c2 | bes,1 | ces2 bes,2 | d1~\> | d1 | R1\! | R1 | }
% c161 (Adler ch. 11, PDF 28 item 3 and Ex. XI-12, Schubert Rosamunde Entr'acte I 106-23: the trombones' diminuendo is scored by dropping two trombones and leaving the third
% to bolster the low strings): 41-42 the bass trombone holds B flat2 with the cellos and Bn 2 into the p of 42 (it stopped with Tbn 1-2 after 40), so the B flat pedal under F1's last head keeps one brass voice to the end of the counterstatement.
pTwoBassTbn = { R1*6 | \tag #'score { R1*2 } \tag #'part { \cueDuring "vc" #UP { R1*2^\markup \tiny "Vc." } } | aes,2.~\ff\> aes,4\f | es2 aes,4 a,4 | bes,1 | f,2 bes,2 | bes,1~\> | bes,1 | R1*2\! | }
% c251 (melody; Adler ch. 11, adler2 PDF 20: Hindemith 'saves the tuba to give fresh color in its presentation of the theme two octaves lower';
%   PDF 33 Ex. XI-16, Wagner, Siegfried III: tuba doubled by bass clarinet and bassoons, the winds slurred, the tuba articulating every note;
%   PDF 33-34: tuba with bassoons, cellos and basses 'one of the most powerful combinations'; PDF 28, Ex. XI-13: the theme in the low choir against heavy counterpoint):
%   27-32 the tuba doubles F1's bass statement at the contrabassoon's pitch (two octaves below the cellos, violas and Hn 3-4), ff with the tutti, tongued (tenuto/staccato, no slur),
%   and rests 33-34 with the contrabassoon before the ff re-entry at 35. The tuba's first melodic notes in the movement; F1's notes and the other parts unchanged.
% c336 (playability; Adler App. A, adler2 PDF 306: the tuba's professional range stops at F1 (8 basso); Ex. X-86, PDF 8: the bottom speaks sluggishly and suits slow-moving bass notes):
% 28.4 the tuba breathes instead of a tongued E flat1 quarter, and 29.1 takes D2 (was D1); the contrabassoon keeps E flat1-D1, the bass clarinet doubles D2. The tuba's other notes in 27-32 are unchanged.
pTwoTuba = { c,2->\ff g,,4.-- aes,,8-- | bes,,4-. aes,,8-. g,,8-. f,,4-. r4 | d,4-- g,,8-. aes,,8-. b,,4-- d,4-- | f,2-> es,4-- d,4-- | es,2-> bes,,4.-- c,8-- | des,4-. c,8-. bes,,8-. aes,,4-. g,,4-. | R1*10 | }
% c326 (playability, timpani; Adler ch. 12, adler2 PDF 79: the timpani strengthen the tonic and dominant bass notes and take part in the climactic cadence points;
%   pedal drums change pitch quickly and easily, which 'has greatly enhanced the function' of the instrument; PDF 81, Ex. XII-51: mark the change of tuning in the rest;
%   PDF 80, Ex. XII-48: a long roll is tied to the stroke where it ends): 39-40 the exposition's half cadence on V of E flat (B flat in every bass part, f >) was the only
%   dominant arrival in the movement without timpani (7-10, 147-152, 219-222 and 261-262 all have them), because the drums are G, A flat, C, E flat. The 28" A flat drum
%   (range F2-C3) is retuned to B flat in the rest at 37 (2.5 bars after its A flat at 36.3) and rolls B flat2 f > p with the contrabassoon, bass trombone and horns 3-4,
%   ending with them at 40.4; the strings carry 41-42 alone into the transition. Back to A flat at 43 (next A flat stroke at 111).
pTwoTimp = { c4->\ff r4 r2 | R1*3 | c4-> r4 r2 | R1 | R1 | g,4-> g,4 g,4 r4 | aes,4->\f r4 r2 | es4 r4 aes,4 r4 | R1^\markup \small \italic "muta A♭ in B♭" | R1 | bes,1:32\f\>~ | bes,2:32~ bes,4:32\p r4 | R1*2 | }
% c225 (MOLA PDF 5: cues during long rests): the harp's first entry is the harmonics at 43 (p) after 42 bars' rest; part-only cue of Violin I 39-42 on the
% right-hand staff (the harmonics are in the left hand). Score and MIDI unchanged (R1*16 as before).
pTwoHarpR = { R1*12 | \tag #'score { R1*4 } \tag #'part { \cueDuring "vlnOne" #UP { R1*4^\markup \tiny "Vln. I" } } | }
